// `Ctor(..)` says "this case carries a payload I'm not reading". On a case that
// carries nothing there is nothing to not-read, so it is refused rather than
// quietly accepted: the two spellings are meant to tell a reader whether the
// case has a payload, and one that lies about it would defeat the point.
variant V = A(i64) | B

fn f(v: V) -> i64 {
    match (v) {
        A(..) => 1,
        B(..) => 2,
    }
}

fn main() -> i64 { f(B) }
--- errors ---
error: constructor "B@V" carries no payload, so ".." ignores nothing — write "B" instead
  --> ./tests/cases/match_arms/err_ignore_payload_nullary.aipl:10:9
   |
10 |         B(..) => 2,
   |         ^
